WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.050
 

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:06.779
Every rock has a story to tell.

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:10.340
No matter what kind of rock, or where you
find it,

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:15.339
it preserves evidence of its history. And
we can learn to decipher that history.

00:00:15.339 --> 00:00:19.270
Take for example, the rock in the Science
Center lobby.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:23.199
This piece of mica schist, now serving as
a wall decoration,

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.910
started its life long before the
dinosaurs. It has undergone many changes

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.369
as it has traveled many miles, both
horizontally and vertically

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:37.450
over hundreds of millions of years. Its
story starts about 450 million years

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:37.899
ago,

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:41.800
on the East coast of the proto-North
American continent, where rivers flowed

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:42.540
into the ocean.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.390
The muddy sediment carried by the rivers

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.489
slowly settled out in the quiet water
beyond the waves.

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:52.670
Over hundreds of thousands of years,

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:57.110
more mud was deposited every year,
gradually accumulating to form a thick

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.810
layer.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:03.400
During this time, the Iapetus Ocean was
gradually decreasing in size,

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.500
because the tectonic plate carrying the
proto African continent

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:12.140
was converging with the proto-North
American plate. The western, leading-edge

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:16.280
the proto- African plate bend down and
subducted underneath the eastern edge of

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.760
the proto-North American plate.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.890
The presence of a deep oceanic trench
along this subduction zone

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.590
allowed a great thickness sediments
eroded from the proto-North American

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:27.200
continent

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.230
to accumulate.

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:33.470
The increasing weight overlying younger
sediments caused the mud to be compacted

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:34.370
and cemented

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:37.560
into a fine-grained sedimentary rock
called shale.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.760
Eventually the Iapetus Ocean floor
was completely subducted,

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.000
allowing proto-Africa to collide with
proto-North America,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.910
forming the super continent of Pangea. Continents have too low a density to be

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:52.720
subducted,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.120
so continent-continent collisions cause
intense deformation of

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:01.570
the sedimentary rocks caught between. Our shale layer was buried even deeper

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:06.180
and folded like an accordion. This
compression cause the clay minerals in

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.509
the shale to be rotated into alignment

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.720
perpendicular to the compression, and the higher temperature at this depth

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.830
caused the clay minerals to re
crystallize into somewhat larger

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:16.760
mica grains,

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.580
forming a metamorphic rock called slate.
Clay minerals and micas

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.050
all have a crystal structure made up of
thin 'sheets' of atoms

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:30.370
that are strongly bonded within each sheet,
but have very weak bonds between

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.720
each sheet. Graphite has a very similar
crystal structure,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.730
which is why it is used in pencils. When
you write on a page with one,

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.640
you brush off the sheets of carbon atoms.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.790
When clay minerals and micas become
strongly aligned in a rock that is squeezed,

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:49.960
the rock itself develops a fine-scale
layering

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:53.090
or foliation along which it tends to
break most easily,

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:57.660
much like the pages in a book

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.310
Thus, the orientation of the foliation in an

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:04.100
ancient metamorphic rock preserves a
record of the plate compression

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:04.770
direction.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:10.130
Continued compression between the two
continents further deformed the rock.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.620
It formed the very high ancestral
Appalachian Mountains,

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:19.170
and also formed correspondingly deep
'roots'. This deformation

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.710
caused the slate to be buried somewhat
deeper, where the temperature was even

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:23.210
greater.

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:28.140
This in turn caused the mica grains to grow even larger in size,

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.240
form this sparkly silver colored
muscovite we see today,

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:34.690
and transforming the rock from a slate
into a mica schist

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:39.860
After the Appalachian Mountains reached
about as high as the present Himalayas,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:43.550
the two continents could not converge
anymore, and so the mountains

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:48.690
began slowly erode. About 200 million
years ago,

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.800
there was a reversal a plate spreading
directions, which caused the

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.160
supercontinent Pangea to rift apart,

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.740
and the present Atlantic Ocean began to
open. The rifting created valleys like

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.020
the African rift valley of

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:05.110
today, and dinosaurs left their footprints
in the mud along lakeshores in

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.400
present day Connecticut Massachusetts.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.940
the Appalachian Mountains continued to
be eroded during this time;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.730
glaciers and rivers and landslides
gradually wore them down,

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.919
lower and lower, transporting sediments
downhill and ultimately depositing them

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:20.760
in the growing

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.370
Atlantic Ocean. As the mountain peaks
were lowered by erosion

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.740
the total mass above ground decreased. To
compensate for this,

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:32.990
the mountain roots rose buoyantly upward,
just like an iceberg

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:38.060
that melts on the top is replenished by the
buoyant rise of it root.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:42.350
Thus over tens of millions of years, the once
deeply buried mica schist

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.890
was passively uplifted closer and closer
to the Earth's surface.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:49.330
The schist was finally exposed at the
surface

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:54.060
within the recent Pleistocene lce Age, when ice sheets originating in northern Canada

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.460
scraped south across New England
multiple times. And then only very

00:04:58.460 --> 00:04:59.130
recently,

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:03.150
a geologist found the mica schist in New
Hampshire, took it back to Brown,

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:07.080
and put together its amazing story.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:07.580
 

